==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|ja|疋|tr=hiki}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|hiki}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} A [[copper]] [[coin]] used in [[Japan]] in the {{w|Sengoku period}}, equal to 10 [[mon]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jerrold M. Packard|title=Sons of Heaven: A Portrait of the Japanese Monarchy|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles Scribner's Sons|Charles Scribner’s Sons]]|year=1987|page=143|isbn=0-684-18633-0|passage=After enough money was at last found, the imperial family was so happy that in appreciation the shogunal official responsible for the release of the burial money—one thousand '''hiki''' (about $5,000)—was granted the coveted right to personally display the imperial crest of chrysanthemum and paulownia.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jin’ichi Konishi|tlr=Aileen Gatten; Mark Harbison|editor=Earl Miner|title=A History of Japanese Literature|volume=3 (The High Middle Ages)|publisher=w:Princeton University Press|year=1991|page=453|isbn=0-691-06592-6|passage=Sogi once donated one thousand '''hiki''' in cash (based on rice prices in 1984, some five thousand dollars) to the court for repairs to the Library of Official Documents (Kammu Bunko).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sōchō|tlr=H. Mack Horton|title=The Journal of Sōchō|publisher=w:Stanford University Press|year=2002|page=130|isbn=0-8047-3506-9|passage=Five hundred '''hiki''' (the cost of five kegs of sake), six loads of charcoal, two baskets of oranges, and various dried foods.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William J. Farge|title=A Christian Samurai: The Trials of Baba Bunkō|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=w:The Catholic University of America Press|year=2016|page=263|isbn=9780813228518|passage=Nakamura was hired that day for the large sum of one thousand '''hiki'''.{{sup|79}} {{...}} 79. Traditionally, the price of one koku of rice was equivalent to one '''hiki''' of silk. In the Edo period one '''hiki''' remained more or less the equivalent of ten mon.}}

